WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.396
This briefing comes from Book 14 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

2
00:00:04.396 --> 00:00:06.787
Federal Compliance and Authorization.

3
00:00:06.787 --> 00:00:09.612
It's written for authorizing officials,

4
00:00:09.612 --> 00:00:13.037
information system security
officers and C I Os.

5
00:00:13.037 --> 00:00:17.558
I'll take four questions: why every
control needs proof rather than a promise,

6
00:00:17.558 --> 00:00:20.931
and what the federal clock now
asks for; what the baseline,

7
00:00:20.931 --> 00:00:24.735
the closure rule and the agency-level
control families require;

8
00:00:24.735 --> 00:00:29.687
who owns compliance; and how to build the
authorization package and keep it current.

9
00:00:29.687 --> 00:00:32.483
The short version: a control
is closed when the thing

10
00:00:32.483 --> 00:00:35.538
that closes it is running and
the record shows it working.

11
00:00:35.538 --> 00:00:39.180
Everything else is a
promise, and promises age.

12
00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:44.217
Federal compliance is usually
described as a list of obligations.

13
00:00:44.217 --> 00:00:48.583
What an authorizing official needs is
narrower: to know that each control

14
00:00:48.583 --> 00:00:52.518
is working today, and to be able to
show anyone who asks how they know.

15
00:00:52.518 --> 00:00:55.067
That's the difference
between a promise and proof.

16
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:57.730
A promise is a statement
in the security plan,

17
00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:01.831
backed by evidence someone gathered
by hand for the last assessment.

18
00:01:01.831 --> 00:01:05.480
It was true the day it was signed,
and it has been aging since.

19
00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:08.099
Proof is a record the
system itself produces,

20
00:01:08.099 --> 00:01:11.780
which another reviewer can check
again and get the same answer.

21
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.230
So start by asking who owes which proof.

22
00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:18.780
The cloud provider proves its
service under FedRAMP's 2026 rules.

23
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.010
The agency proves its own system
and its side of the boundary:

24
00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:26.817
its configuration, identities,
data and integrations.

25
00:01:26.817 --> 00:01:30.933
FedRAMP is plain that each agency
still authorizes its own systems,

26
00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:35.268
and should never issue a standalone
authorization for a cloud service.

27
00:01:35.268 --> 00:01:40.234
OMB's M-24-15 gives a provider's
package a presumption of adequacy,

28
00:01:40.234 --> 00:01:45.293
but the authorizing official must
still issue an authorization.

29
00:01:45.293 --> 00:01:49.760
And only that official decides whether
the remaining risk is acceptable.

30
00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:53.837
The dates that drive this
work belong to FedRAMP,

31
00:01:53.837 --> 00:01:56.997
CISA and OMB, and each
binds a different party.

32
00:01:56.997 --> 00:02:03.222
Every date here was checked against its
issuing document on October 21, 2026.

33
00:02:03.222 --> 00:02:07.935
Where a document gives a period rather
than a date, the date is counted from it.

34
00:02:07.935 --> 00:02:13.853
The nearest is OMB's M-26-15:
each agency's post-quantum

35
00:02:13.853 --> 00:02:20.873
migration plan is due within 120
days, which is October 22, 2026.

36
00:02:20.873 --> 00:02:25.624
The same memorandum sets TLS
1.3 support by January 2, 2030,

37
00:02:25.624 --> 00:02:31.285
and mitigation of as much quantum
risk as feasible by the end of 2030.

38
00:02:31.285 --> 00:02:38.248
Under M-26-14, on logging, the Agency
Logging Plan falls on November 18.

39
00:02:38.248 --> 00:02:43.981
On December 7, CISA's BOD 26-04 holds
agencies to its remediation timelines,

40
00:02:43.981 --> 00:02:49.160
and FedRAMP's vulnerability rules
become mandatory for providers.

41
00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:54.503
FedRAMP's 2026 rules take mandatory
effect on January 1, 2027,

42
00:02:54.503 --> 00:03:00.060
and listed end-of-support edge
devices must be out by February 5.

43
00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.235
Some things moved.

44
00:03:01.235 --> 00:03:07.329
M-26-05 made software bills of materials
and attestation forms contract choices,

45
00:03:07.329 --> 00:03:10.785
but kept the software
and hardware inventory.

46
00:03:10.785 --> 00:03:15.240
So keep one dated register,
and re-verify it at every gate.

47
00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:20.865
Most civilian cloud systems are authorized
at Moderate, and the word does real work.

48
00:03:20.865 --> 00:03:26.200
Under FIPS 199, the agency rates the
impact of losing confidentiality,

49
00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:30.615
integrity and availability
for each kind of information.

50
00:03:30.615 --> 00:03:32.816
FIPS 200 takes the high-water mark:

51
00:03:32.816 --> 00:03:37.690
at least one objective at moderate and
none at high makes a moderate system.

52
00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:41.972
The agency then selects and
tailors NIST's Moderate baseline,

53
00:03:41.972 --> 00:03:46.590
which in the current release holds
287 controls and enhancements.

54
00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.702
That's the size of the promise
a Moderate system makes.

55
00:03:49.702 --> 00:03:52.440
FedRAMP's Class C is a separate fact.

56
00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:57.635
It includes the current Moderate
baseline, but FedRAMP lists 322 controls

57
00:03:57.635 --> 00:04:02.327
and enhancements for it, 36 of them
outside NIST's Moderate baseline.

58
00:04:02.327 --> 00:04:06.301
Automated access enforcement
for changes, for example,

59
00:04:06.301 --> 00:04:09.755
is NIST High only, yet
FedRAMP lists it for Class C.

60
00:04:09.755 --> 00:04:12.778
Non-repudiation is High
only on both lists.

61
00:04:12.778 --> 00:04:14.515
So cite the two separately.

62
00:04:14.515 --> 00:04:17.709
A Moderate system built on
a Class C service inherits

63
00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:20.540
the provider's evidence
for the provider's side.

64
00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:25.340
It still has to prove its own side,
against its own tailored baseline.

65
00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:27.515
Is a control closed?

66
00:04:27.515 --> 00:04:32.098
A program that can't answer precisely
answers loosely: the product was bought,

67
00:04:32.098 --> 00:04:35.965
the vendor announced the feature,
the ticket was marked complete.

68
00:04:35.965 --> 00:04:38.165
None of those is the control operating.

69
00:04:38.165 --> 00:04:40.665
The book applies two tests, in order.

70
00:04:40.665 --> 00:04:45.479
First, the mechanism exists: the thing
that closes the control is deployed,

71
00:04:45.479 --> 00:04:49.503
running, and reaches the part of
the estate the control covers.

72
00:04:49.503 --> 00:04:53.140
A purchase order, an ended
pilot or a roadmap fails.

73
00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:58.734
Second, its proof is recorded: the closure
carries a machine-readable verification,

74
00:04:58.734 --> 00:05:01.490
a time, and the identity
of what checked it.

75
00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:04.086
A control that fails
the first test is open,

76
00:05:04.086 --> 00:05:07.128
and belongs in the plan of
action with a milestone.

77
00:05:07.128 --> 00:05:10.716
One that passes the first but
fails the second is worse:

78
00:05:10.716 --> 00:05:13.189
it looks closed and can't be shown to be.

79
00:05:13.189 --> 00:05:17.825
A person clicking complete is an
exception, flagged and counted separately,

80
00:05:17.825 --> 00:05:21.577
and reports are derived from
closure records, never compiled.

81
00:05:21.577 --> 00:05:24.028
And a vendor roadmap is not a commitment.

82
00:05:24.028 --> 00:05:28.322
Date every claim, confirm it before
a plan or contract rests on it,

83
00:05:28.322 --> 00:05:31.600
and write the capability
into the contract.

84
00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:35.712
Some control families no cloud
provider can close for you.

85
00:05:35.712 --> 00:05:37.663
The first is program management.

86
00:05:37.663 --> 00:05:41.687
NIST marks the whole family as
deployed organization-wide,

87
00:05:41.687 --> 00:05:45.959
outside the system baselines
and independent of impact level,

88
00:05:45.959 --> 00:05:50.724
and FedRAMP lists no class for any
of its 37 controls and enhancements.

89
00:05:50.724 --> 00:05:55.021
The obligation comes from
FISMA and OMB's Circular A-130,

90
00:05:55.021 --> 00:06:00.250
and it binds the agency whether or
not it uses a single cloud service.

91
00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:03.712
No resolver, overlay or
enforcement point can write an

92
00:06:03.712 --> 00:06:07.713
organization's risk tolerance
or appoint its senior official.

93
00:06:07.713 --> 00:06:12.416
These controls close when the artifact
exists, a named official has approved it,

94
00:06:12.416 --> 00:06:16.549
and the agency can show it is still
followed: the security program plan,

95
00:06:16.549 --> 00:06:20.111
the appointed senior agency
information security officer,

96
00:06:20.111 --> 00:06:24.671
a risk strategy with a signed tolerance
statement, a process that keeps every

97
00:06:24.671 --> 00:06:29.588
system's plan of action comparable, and
one organization-wide monitoring strategy.

98
00:06:29.588 --> 00:06:31.876
One distinction matters to an assessor.

99
00:06:31.876 --> 00:06:34.284
The program inventory lists systems;

100
00:06:34.284 --> 00:06:37.937
each system's component
inventory lists what's inside it.

101
00:06:37.937 --> 00:06:39.580
The two must agree.

102
00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:43.868
Two more families close through
governance rather than technology.

103
00:06:43.868 --> 00:06:47.492
Contingency planning asks
for a plan, not a capability.

104
00:06:47.492 --> 00:06:51.550
An agency can have tested failover,
two circuits and good backups,

105
00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:54.630
and still carry an open
contingency plan finding,

106
00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:58.387
because nothing says who declares
a contingency, who is told,

107
00:06:58.387 --> 00:07:02.068
what comes back first, or when
operations are back to normal.

108
00:07:02.068 --> 00:07:06.275
The business impact analysis sets
the numbers, and the mission owner,

109
00:07:06.275 --> 00:07:10.556
not the database administrator, sets
the maximum tolerable downtime.

110
00:07:10.556 --> 00:07:14.293
For a Moderate system, NIST's
contingency guide recommends a

111
00:07:14.293 --> 00:07:18.480
functional exercise with a restore
from backup, not just a tabletop.

112
00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:20.218
Supply chain moved this year.

113
00:07:20.218 --> 00:07:25.931
M-26-05 rescinded the
secure-development attestation mandate.

114
00:07:25.931 --> 00:07:29.238
The software and hardware
inventory is still required;

115
00:07:29.238 --> 00:07:33.337
software bills of materials are now
a contract choice, and for a cloud

116
00:07:33.337 --> 00:07:37.580
platform the one to ask for covers
the runtime production environment.

117
00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:40.598
Even so, a supply chain
policy, plan, controls,

118
00:07:40.598 --> 00:07:45.880
acquisition terms and notification
agreements are in every NIST baseline.

119
00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:49.170
And FedRAMP's vulnerability
rules bind providers;

120
00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:53.300
the agency reads their reports
into its own plan of action.

121
00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:57.475
Privacy and training are
the agency's, end to end.

122
00:07:57.475 --> 00:08:01.926
NIST's privacy baseline applies whatever
the impact level, so a Low system

123
00:08:01.926 --> 00:08:06.450
holding the same personal information
carries the same privacy controls.

124
00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:09.572
No privacy control
carries a FedRAMP class,

125
00:08:09.572 --> 00:08:13.475
and none of the 2026 indicator
themes is about privacy.

126
00:08:13.475 --> 00:08:17.581
The controls ask by what authority
the agency collects the information,

127
00:08:17.581 --> 00:08:20.045
for what purpose, whether
it told the person,

128
00:08:20.045 --> 00:08:23.125
and whether it published the
system of records notice.

129
00:08:23.125 --> 00:08:25.605
The senior agency official for privacy

130
00:08:25.605 --> 00:08:28.687
approves the privacy plan
before authorization.

131
00:08:28.687 --> 00:08:32.513
Training is the one family
no configuration can close.

132
00:08:32.513 --> 00:08:36.657
OMB's Circular A-130 requires
training before access,

133
00:08:36.657 --> 00:08:40.425
and OPM's rule sets an
annual floor for awareness.

134
00:08:40.425 --> 00:08:43.994
Social engineering awareness
is required at Moderate;

135
00:08:43.994 --> 00:08:47.563
phishing exercises are
optional, tailored in by choice.

136
00:08:47.563 --> 00:08:52.599
A dashboard showing 98 percent completion
is a number with no denominator.

137
00:08:52.599 --> 00:08:56.573
The proof is counts per population,
measured against a stated roster,

138
00:08:56.573 --> 00:08:59.870
which someone who didn't
produce them can re-derive.

139
00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:04.745
Ask who owns compliance and the
answer is often the security office,

140
00:09:04.745 --> 00:09:06.808
which means nobody in particular.

141
00:09:06.808 --> 00:09:10.619
The Risk Management Framework
names an owner for every task.

142
00:09:10.619 --> 00:09:15.373
The system owner owns more of those
tasks than any other role: categorizing,

143
00:09:15.373 --> 00:09:19.283
selecting and implementing
controls, remediating findings,

144
00:09:19.283 --> 00:09:22.733
keeping the plan of action,
and assembling the package.

145
00:09:22.733 --> 00:09:27.779
The CISO sets control requirements and
the common controls that systems inherit,

146
00:09:27.779 --> 00:09:31.508
and reviews providers' quarterly
reports or names a delegate.

147
00:09:31.508 --> 00:09:34.842
The privacy official approves
the categorization and

148
00:09:34.842 --> 00:09:38.320
the privacy plan of any system
with personal information.

149
00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:42.339
The authorizing official is the only
official who can accept the risk,

150
00:09:42.339 --> 00:09:44.557
and that acceptance can't be delegated.

151
00:09:44.557 --> 00:09:49.678
The official, or the designated representative,
also approves the categorization,

152
00:09:49.678 --> 00:09:51.756
the plans and the assessment plan,

153
00:09:51.756 --> 00:09:55.170
and selects the assessor,
before the assessment starts.

154
00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:57.315
Six roles are government-only,

155
00:09:57.315 --> 00:10:01.433
among them the authorizing
official, the C I O and the CISO.

156
00:10:01.433 --> 00:10:05.331
A contractor-heavy team can
prepare every plan and package,

157
00:10:05.331 --> 00:10:07.320
but can't sign those decisions.

158
00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:10.809
And keep the roles apart:
nobody assesses their own work,

159
00:10:10.809 --> 00:10:14.430
and the authorizing official
isn't the system owner.

160
00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:18.593
The authorizing official doesn't
read the evidence directly.

161
00:10:18.593 --> 00:10:20.804
They read a package that summarizes it.

162
00:10:20.804 --> 00:10:25.748
NIST defines the package by its contents:
the security and privacy plans, the

163
00:10:25.748 --> 00:10:31.005
assessment reports, the plans of action
and milestones, and an executive summary.

164
00:10:31.005 --> 00:10:34.817
Each comes from a Risk Management
Framework task with a named owner.

165
00:10:34.817 --> 00:10:38.471
NIST also says the package
may be generated from a tool,

166
00:10:38.471 --> 00:10:43.341
and FedRAMP's 2026 agency rules
require governance and inventory tools

167
00:10:43.341 --> 00:10:48.455
that produce and ingest machine-readable
artifacts, at least OSCAL and JSON.

168
00:10:48.455 --> 00:10:51.699
OSCAL gives each artifact a
model, and the models link:

169
00:10:51.699 --> 00:10:55.976
the assessment plan imports the
security plan, the results import the

170
00:10:55.976 --> 00:11:00.105
assessment plan, and the plan of
action hangs from the security plan.

171
00:11:00.105 --> 00:11:02.643
Every failing item gets a disposition.

172
00:11:02.643 --> 00:11:06.743
Actionable drift is an open item
with an owner and a deadline.

173
00:11:06.743 --> 00:11:10.792
A governed exception carries an
in-date acceptance and its expiry.

174
00:11:10.792 --> 00:11:13.005
A lapsed acceptance reverts to open.

175
00:11:13.005 --> 00:11:14.830
Nothing is always satisfied.

176
00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:18.904
And an empty plan of action on a Moderate
system should prompt a question:

177
00:11:18.904 --> 00:11:21.170
what didn't the assessment look at?

178
00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:25.358
A package describes the system
on the day it was generated.

179
00:11:25.358 --> 00:11:28.082
Continuous monitoring
keeps that day recent.

180
00:11:28.082 --> 00:11:30.008
The law already sets the floor.

181
00:11:30.008 --> 00:11:32.891
FISMA requires testing
no less than annually,

182
00:11:32.891 --> 00:11:35.244
and there's no federal three-year rule.

183
00:11:35.244 --> 00:11:41.058
OMB's Circular A-130 tells agencies to move
to ongoing authorization when a system

184
00:11:41.058 --> 00:11:44.563
is eligible, and once
monitoring is mature enough,

185
00:11:44.563 --> 00:11:47.470
a review frequency replaces the end date.

186
00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:51.933
NIST is clear that controls which can't
be automated must still be assessed.

187
00:11:51.933 --> 00:11:56.527
The program's cadence, as its own
design: monthly, regenerate the results

188
00:11:56.527 --> 00:12:01.045
and the plan of action, and report
posture to the authorizing official.

189
00:12:01.045 --> 00:12:05.044
Quarterly, review each provider's
Ongoing Certification Report;

190
00:12:05.044 --> 00:12:09.932
FedRAMP's rule says agencies must, and they
should name a senior official to do it.

191
00:12:09.932 --> 00:12:13.994
Annually, the independent assessor
re-assesses every control.

192
00:12:13.994 --> 00:12:17.525
Agencies must also notify
FedRAMP when a report raises

193
00:12:17.525 --> 00:12:21.208
a concern likely to lead to
rescinding the authorization.

194
00:12:21.208 --> 00:12:24.294
And vendor posture scores
aren't assessment evidence.

195
00:12:24.294 --> 00:12:27.845
A score measures progress against
a vendor's recommendations.

196
00:12:27.845 --> 00:12:32.920
An assessment result says whether an
objective is satisfied, and on what evidence.

197
00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:37.337
The Federal Compliance series
arranged its material by layer;

198
00:12:37.337 --> 00:12:40.933
the workbook books arrange it by
the question a reader brings.

199
00:12:40.933 --> 00:12:46.181
Chapter 12 maps each part of the series
to the book that carries it now,

200
00:12:46.181 --> 00:12:48.944
and Appendix A indexes 149 controls.

201
00:12:48.944 --> 00:12:54.345
Where a book and the older series disagree,
the book is the later, footnoted text.

202
00:12:54.345 --> 00:12:56.783
Two dependency claims explain the map.

203
00:12:56.783 --> 00:13:01.621
The naming and identity floor comes first:
you can't inventory what you can't name.

204
00:13:01.621 --> 00:13:04.306
Assurance comes last,
because the package can

205
00:13:04.306 --> 00:13:07.345
only report what the earlier
books made observable.

206
00:13:07.345 --> 00:13:12.728
Build everything on the map and risk still
remains, in six kinds: self-assessment,

207
00:13:12.728 --> 00:13:17.377
until an independent assessor confirms
it; what automation can't see;

208
00:13:17.377 --> 00:13:21.536
risk inherited from providers;
evidence that stops arriving;

209
00:13:21.536 --> 00:13:25.451
risks outside control evaluation,
such as insider threat;

210
00:13:25.451 --> 00:13:28.958
and design not yet built, or
features not yet shipped.

211
00:13:28.958 --> 00:13:30.294
Write the remainder down.

212
00:13:30.294 --> 00:13:33.114
A package that lists
what's proven, asserted,

213
00:13:33.114 --> 00:13:38.340
inherited and accepted gives the
authorizing official a risk they can see.

214
00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:40.465
So, four decisions.

215
00:13:40.465 --> 00:13:45.297
Name the owners in writing: signed
appointments, a person in every decide and

216
00:13:45.297 --> 00:13:50.053
do cell of the matrix, and the authorizing
official selecting the assessor.

217
00:13:50.053 --> 00:13:51.614
Keep one dated clock.

218
00:13:51.614 --> 00:13:54.368
Submit the post-quantum migration plan,

219
00:13:54.368 --> 00:13:58.790
and re-verify every federal date
against its source at each gate.

220
00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:02.940
Fund the mechanisms that produce records
before the tools that measure them.

221
00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:06.765
Assessment tools confirm a design;
they can't stand in for one.

222
00:14:06.765 --> 00:14:11.479
And generate the package: machine-readable
records, a disposition on every

223
00:14:11.479 --> 00:14:16.040
failing item, and a named reviewer
for every provider's quarterly report.

224
00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:18.240
The next step needs no procurement.

225
00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:22.453
Fill the product inventory questionnaire
against the estate as it is,

226
00:14:22.453 --> 00:14:25.504
verify each FedRAMP status
on the day you record it,

227
00:14:25.504 --> 00:14:28.990
and decide every flag before
the security plan is written.

228
00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:31.428
A promise is true on the day it's signed.

229
